ery name against `release.yml` and `.goreleaser.yaml` before writing.\n4. `docs/proposal.md`:\n   - Guardrails (`## Guardrails`, after the \"tag-writing credential is separate\" bullet, ~line 526): add a bullet \"**Gatekeeper: unsigned binary, quarantine cleared (accepted risk).**\" saying the release binary is neither signed nor notarized, the cask removes `com.apple.quarantine` from the installed binary in `hooks.post.install`, this skips Gatekeeper's check so the install path trusts the release archive and its checksum (and the tap token holder), the owner accepts it, and Apple notarization (Developer ID signing in the release workflow) is the follow-up. Flag the wording for maintainer confirmation in the PR body.\n   - Rollout (~line 582): Phase 5 sentence exists. Expand lightly only if it lacks the install docs: say Phase 5 ships the README Install section and release runbook. Do not touch the mermaid diagram.\n   - Delivery (~line 565): already complete; only add a pointer \"Install instructions: README\" if useful. No other change.\n\n### Test plan\n\n- Command: `make lint test` (docs-only; confirms nothing else broke). If a docs link or markdown check exists in the Makefile/CI, run it (`grep -n docs Makefile`).\n- Manual checks (the worker reports these in the PR):\n  - Every command, file, secret, variable and workflow name in the new text appears verbatim in `release.yml`, `version.yml` or `.goreleaser.yaml` (`grep` each).\n  - README and the runbook say `hooks.post.install`, not `postflight`.\n  - README link anchors resolve (`docs/proposal.md#guardrails`).\n  - `git diff --stat` shows only `README.md`, `docs/site/install.md`, `docs/runbooks/release.md`, `docs/proposal.md`; existing runbook sections are unchanged apart from additions.\n  - Run `make release-check` only if goreleaser is installed locally; it is not required.\n- Failure case: if docs tests (e.g. in `docs/site` build or `internal/` tests that read docs) fail, fix wording, not the tests.\n\n### Risk\n\nLow. Docs only; none of the protected paths (`.ard/`, `.github/`) are touched. No public interface or migration. The only sensitive part is the Gatekeeper accepted-risk wording, which a maintainer should confirm in review.\n\n### Size\n\nS to M (roughly 60 to 120 changed lines). Files: `README.md`, `docs/site/install.md`, `docs/runbooks/release.md`, `docs/proposal.md`.\n138\n142\n157\n158\n159\n160\n161\n165\n168\n174\n178\n184\n194\n203\n204\n205\n212\n213\n215\n216\n217\n221\n222\n223\n224\n227\n231\n236\n237\n238\n239\n241\n242\n243\n244\n245\n247\n252\n254\n255\n267\n268\n276\n277\n292\n293\n297\n298\n299\n300\n301\n302\n304\n305\n306\n307\n312\n313\n314\n315\n316\n317\n318\n321\n322\n323\n332\n333\n334\n335\n336\n337\n338\n339\n340\n344\n345\n346\n347\n348\n349\n350\n355\n357\n366\n384\n385\n386\n387\n388\n394\n396\n40\n402\n406\n407\n41\n410\n411\n413\n414\n415\n416\n42\n426\n43\n435\n44\n440\n441\n449\n45\n47\n48\n49\n50\n51\n52\n56\n58\n59\n61\n89\nconfig.yaml\nreviewers\nscanners\ntriage\nworkers","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VeyaWUdGp6NWu7azedP2WA","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChS4a4dx28xrNRMpmdZkH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012MQspFanwvNyX8orGWjP3U","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat docs/runbooks/release.md; cat docs/site/install.md; grep -n '^## ' README.md; cat .github/workflows/release.yml; grep -n -B2 -A25 'cask\\|release:' .goreleaser.yaml | head -120; grep -n -i 'guardrail\\|tag-writing\\|Phase 5' docs/proposal.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5420,"cache_read_input_tokens":11562,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5420},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7eb1afcb-ed8c-476d-bb5f-6257ca4c1350","uuid":"c65811da-ee19-4ef1-b06f-36ce6c5dd237","timestamp":"2026-11-26T19:06:02.270Z","request_id":"req_011ChS4a4W2MwZwL8oH2Taxq"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012MQspFanwvNyX8orGWjP3U","type":"tool_result","content":"# Release runbook\n\n`.github/workflows/version.yml` tags every first-parent merge to `main` as `vX.Y.Z` and creates a notes-only release. This runbook covers setup and recovery. Names come from the workflow.\n\n## Seed tag\n\nAn admin creates the annotated seed tag once, on a commit on `main`:\n\n```\ngit tag -a v0.1.0 <sha> -m v0.1.0\ngit push origin refs/tags/v0.1.0\n```\n\nUntil it exists a dry run plans nothing and a live run fails.\n\n## Settings checklist\n\n- Repository variables: `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (ard's App login, `<slug>[bot]`), `ARD_VERSIONING` (`on` for live), `RELEASE_APP_ID`, `RELEASE_BOT_NAME`, `RELEASE_BOT_EMAIL`.\n- A `release` environment holding the secret `RELEASE_APP_PRIVATE_KEY`. The release App needs contents: write only, and must not be ard's App.\n- A tag ruleset on `v*` that only the release App may bypass.\n- For Homebrew: the secret `TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN` in the same `release` environment, and the `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap` repository (see Homebrew release).\n\nThe steps below set these up once. They use `ard-release` as the App's name; use any name that is free, and substitute it throughout.\n\n## Create the release App\n\n`version.yml`'s `tag` job mints a short-lived token from this App (`actions/create-github-app-token`) to push tags and create releases. It is a separate App from ard's so that ard's own token can never create a tag; the job refuses to run if the two are the same (it compares the App's slug with `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`).\n\n1. Open <https://github.com/settings/apps/new> (for an organization: the organization's Settings → Developer settings → GitHub Apps → New GitHub App).\n   - **GitHub App name:** `ard-release`. **Homepage URL:** the repository URL.\n   - **Webhook:** clear **Active**. The App receives no events.\n   - **Repository permissions:** **Contents: Read and write**. Leave every other permission at **No access** (Metadata stays read-only, as GitHub requires).\n   - **Where can this GitHub App be installed?** **Only on this account**.\n   - Select **Create GitHub App**.\n2. On the App's page, note the **App ID**. Under **Private keys**, select **Generate a private key**; the browser downloads a `.pem` file.\n3. Select **Install App**, choose the account, then **Only select repositories** → `albertcbrown/ard`. Install it on nothing else.\n4. Set the repository variables. The tagger email is the bot's noreply address, which needs the bot account's numeric user ID:\n\n   ```\n   gh api '/users/ard-release[bot]' -q .id\n   gh variable set RELEASE_APP_ID    -R albertcbrown/ard -b '<app-id>'\n   gh variable set RELEASE_BOT_NAME  -R albertcbrown/ard -b 'ard-release[bot]'\n   gh variable set RELEASE_BOT_EMAIL -R albertcbrown/ard -b '<bot-user-id>+ard-release[bot]@users.noreply.github.com'\n   ```\n\n   Check that `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` is also set to ard's own App login (`gh variable list -R albertcbrown/ard`).\n5. Create the `release` environment and store the key only there, then delete the downloaded file:\n\n   ```\n   gh api -X PUT repos/albertcbrown/ard/environments/release\n   gh secret set RELEASE_APP_PRIVATE_KEY -R albertcbrown/ard --env release < ~/Downloads/ard-release.<date>.private-key.pem\n   rm ~/Downloads/ard-release.<date>.private-key.pem\n   ```\n\n   Optionally restrict the environment's deployment branches and tags to `main` and `v*` (Settings → Environments → release), so only runs from those refs can read its secrets.\n\nTo rotate the key, generate a new one on the App page, run the `gh secret set` line again with it, then delete the old key on the App page.\n\n## Protect the tags\n\nA tag ruleset keeps tags immutable and lets only the release App create them. `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`'s App, workflows using `GITHUB_TOKEN` and people without bypass cannot create, move or delete a `v*` tag.\n\n```\ngh api -X POST repos/albertcbrown/ard/rulesets --input - <<'EOF'\n{\n  \"name\": \"release tags\",\n  \"target\": \"tag\",\n  \"enforcement\": \"active\",\n  \"conditions\": { \"ref_name\": { \"include\": [\"refs/tags/v*\"], \"exclude\": [] } },\n  \"rules\": [ { \"type\": \"creation\" }, { \"type\": \"update\" }, { \"type\": \"deletion\" } ],\n  \"bypass_actors\": [ { \"actor_id\": <app-id>, \"actor_type\": \"Integration\", \"bypass_mode\": \"always\" } ]\n}\nEOF\n```\n\n`actor_id` is the release App's **App ID** (the same number as `RELEASE_APP_ID`). The same ruleset can be made in Settings → Rules → Rulesets → New tag ruleset: target `v*`, enable **Restrict creations**, **Restrict updates** and **Restrict deletions**, and add the App under **Bypass list**. Check it with `gh api repos/albertcbrown/ard/rulesets`.\n\nThe seed tag (above) must exist before the ruleset, or an admin must be added to the bypass list while creating it and removed afterwards.\n\n## Turn it on\n\n1. Run a dry run and read the plan in the run's step summary:\n\n   ```\n   gh workflow run version.yml -R albertcbrown/ard\n   gh run watch -R albertcbrown/ard \"$(gh run list -R albertcbrown/ard -w version.yml -L 1 --json databaseId -q '.[0].databaseId')\"\n   ```\n\n2. Set `ARD_VERSIONING` to `on` (`gh variable set ARD_VERSIONING -R albertcbrown/ard -b on`).\n3. Run live, raising `max_tags` when the dry run planned more than 20 tags:\n\n   ```\n   gh workflow run version.yml -R albertcbrown/ard -f dry_run=false -f max_tags=60\n   ```\n\n   A failure that names a variable or the App points at the step above that sets it; fix it and rerun (see Recovery).\n\n## Homebrew release (release.yml)\n\n`.github/workflows/release.yml` runs on each `v*` tag `version.yml` pushes (and on `workflow_dispatch` with a `tag`). It builds the four archives with GoReleaser (`.goreleaser.yaml`), uploads them and `checksums.txt` to the tag's existing release, and updates `Casks/ard.rb` in `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap`.\n\n- It does nothing until `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on` **and** the `release` environment holds the secret `TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN`. Without the token it logs a notice and stops.\n- `TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN`: a fine-grained token (or a second App's token) with **contents: read and write on `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap` only**. Store it only in the `release` environment.\n- Create `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap` (public, with a `Casks/` directory) and protect its `main`.\n\nTo set up the tap and its token:\n\n1. Create the tap repository with a `Casks/` directory:\n\n   ```\n   gh repo create albertcbrown/homebrew-tap --public --description \"Homebrew tap for ard\"\n   gh repo clone albertcbrown/homebrew-tap && cd homebrew-tap\n   mkdir Casks && touch Casks/.gitkeep && git add Casks && git commit -m \"Add Casks directory\" && git push origin HEAD:main\n   ```\n\n2. Create the token at <https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens/new>: **Resource owner** `albertcbrown`, **Repository access** → **Only select repositories** → `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap`, **Repository permissions** → **Contents: Read and write**, nothing else. Pick an expiry and note it; the release stops with a push error when it lapses.\n3. Store it in the `release` environment only. `gh` prompts for the value, so it stays out of shell history:\n\n   ```\n   gh secret set TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN -R albertcbrown/ard --env release\n   ```\n\n4. Protect the tap's `main` (Settings → Rules → New branch ruleset: block force pushes and deletions). GoReleaser commits `Casks/ard.rb` directly, so do not require pull requests there.\n5. Rotate the token before it expires: create a new one and run step 3 again, then revoke the old one.\n- On a tag push only the newest `v*` tag is released, so a backfill of many tags publishes once. To build an older tag: `gh workflow run release.yml -R albertcbrown/ard -f tag=vX.Y.Z`.\n- One release runs at a time (`concurrency: release`, never cancelled).\n- Check locally first: `make release-check` (snapshot, publishes nothing).\n\n## Dry run to live\n\nEvery run is a dry run until `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on`. Check the plan in a dry run's step summary, then set the variable. `workflow_dispatch` is a dry run unless `dry_run` is false; `max_tags` (default 20) caps the tags one run creates, so raise it after reading the plan when a backlog is larger.\n\n## Bumps and the module path\n\nLabel a PR `semver:minor` or `semver:major`; with neither it is a patch, and with both major wins. Go requires a `/vN` module path for N >= 2. A `semver:major` that would reach v2 stops the run (exit 2) until `go.mod` says `module .../v2`; relabel the PR or change `go.mod`, then rerun.\n\n## Recovery\n\n- A failed run stops at the first failure. Rerun it: the plan is rebuilt from the tags that exist, and a tag without a release gets a release-only backfill row.\n- Never delete or move a tag. If a tag is wrong, fix forward with the next version.\n- `ard version` prints the tag after `make build` on a tagged commit, and after `go install github.com/albertcbrown/ard/cmd/ard@vX.Y.Z`.\n# Install\n\nard runs on macOS and Linux. It needs:\n\n- Go (to build), `make` and `git` 2.47.0 or newer. An older git fails some fetches.\n- The `claude` CLI for the default agent.\n- Docker, only for roles that ship a `Dockerfile` (the cve, docs and lint scanners).\n\nBuild from source:\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard\ncd ard\nmake build\n./bin/ard version\n```\n\nPut `bin/ard` on your `PATH`. A Homebrew cask is planned (#177); this page will point to it once it lands.\n9:## How it works\n26:## Configuration by convention\n42:## Getting started\n46:## Development\n58:## License\n# Release: build ard with GoReleaser and attach the archives and checksums to\n# the notes-only release version.yml created for a v* tag, then update the\n# Homebrew cask in albertcbrown/homebrew-tap.\n#\n# Inert until switched on: it does nothing unless the repository variable\n# ARD_VERSIONING is \"on\" and the release environment holds TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN\n# (a token that can push to albertcbrown/homebrew-tap only). Without the token\n# it says so and stops; it never half-publishes.\n#\n# Only the newest v* tag is released on a tag push, so version.yml's backfill\n# of many historical tags does not publish one release per tag. Use\n# workflow_dispatch with a tag to build an older one on purpose.\n#\n# Settings and recovery: docs/runbooks/release.md. Design: docs/proposal.md\n# (Delivery) and #177.\nname: release\n\non:\n  push:\n    tags: ['v*']\n  workflow_dispatch:\n    inputs:\n      tag:\n        description: Tag to release (vX.Y.Z); must already have a release\n        required: true\n        type: string\n\n# One release at a time; never cancel one that is uploading.\nconcurrency:\n  group: release\n  cancel-in-progress: false\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\njobs:\n  release:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 30\n    environment: release\n    permissions:\n      contents: write # upload assets to the existing release only\n    env:\n      ENABLED: ${{ vars.ARD_VERSIONING }}\n      EVENT: ${{ github.event_name }}\n      PUSHED_TAG: ${{ github.ref_name }}\n      INPUT_TAG: ${{ inputs.tag }}\n      HAS_TAP_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN != '' }}\n    steps:\n      - name: Decide\n        id: decide\n        shell: bash\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          skip() { echo \"::notice title=release::$1\"; echo \"run=false\" >>\"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"; exit 0; }\n          [[ \"${ENABLED:-}\" == on ]] || skip \"ARD_VERSIONING is not on; nothing to release\"\n          [[ \"${HAS_TAP_TOKEN:-}\" == true ]] || skip \"the release environment has no TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN; create the tap token first (docs/runbooks/release.md)\"\n          if [[ \"$EVENT\" == workflow_dispatch ]]; then tag=$INPUT_TAG; else tag=$PUSHED_TAG; fi\n          [[ \"$tag\" =~ ^v(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)$ ]] || { echo \"::error title=release::not a vX.Y.Z tag: $tag\"; exit 2; }\n          echo \"tag=$tag\" >>\"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n          echo \"run=true\" >>\"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n\n      - uses: actions/checkout@11d5960a326750d5838078e36cf38b85af677262 # v4.4.0\n        if: steps.decide.outputs.run == 'true'\n        with:\n          ref: refs/tags/${{ steps.decide.outputs.tag }}\n          fetch-depth: 0\n          fetch-tags: true\n          persist-credentials: false\n\n      - name: Newest tag only on push\n        id: newest\n        if: steps.decide.outputs.run == 'true'\n        shell: bash\n        env:\n          TAG: ${{ steps.decide.outputs.tag }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          newest=$(git tag -l 'v*' | grep -E '^v(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)$' | sort -V | tail -n 1)\n          if [[ \"$EVENT\" == push && \"$TAG\" != \"$newest\" ]]; then\n            echo \"::notice title=release::$TAG is not the newest tag ($newest); skipping (dispatch with tag=$TAG to build it)\"\n            echo \"run=false\" >>\"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n          else\n            echo \"run=true\" >>\"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n          fi\n\n      - name: Release exists\n        if: steps.newest.outputs.run == 'true'\n        shell: bash\n        env:\n          GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}\n          TAG: ${{ steps.decide.outputs.tag }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          gh release view \"$TAG\" -R \"$GITHUB_REPOSITORY\" --json tagName >/dev/null \\\n            || { echo \"::error title=release::no release for $TAG; version.yml creates it first\"; exit 2; }\n\n      - uses: actions/setup-go@d8cbb8045d7f43706b9177cceb36c1f91a6644d5 # v7.1.0\n        if: steps.newest.outputs.run == 'true'\n        with:\n          go-version-file: go.mod\n          cache: false\n\n      - uses: goreleaser/goreleaser-action@a4f02ab5355a589de60d917a659ce617ed19b3de # v7.3.0\n        if: steps.newest.outputs.run == 'true'\n        with:\n          distribution: goreleaser\n          version: v2.20.3\n          args: release --clean\n        env:\n          GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}\n          TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN }}\n          GORELEASER_CURRENT_TAG: ${{ steps.decide.outputs.tag }}\n27-\n28-# version.yml already created the (notes-only) release; add files to it.\n29:release:\n30-  mode: keep-existing\n31-  github:\n32-    owner: albertcbrown\n33-    name: ard\n34-\n35:homebrew_casks:\n36-  - name: ard\n37-    directory: Casks\n38-    homepage: https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard\n39-    description: Autonomous repository development agents driven by GitHub issues\n40-    # The token comes from the environment of the release workflow.\n41-    repository:\n42-      owner: albertcbrown\n43-      name: homebrew-tap\n44-      token: \"{{ .Env.TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN }}\"\n45-    url:\n46-      template: \"https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/releases/download/{{ .Tag }}/{{ .ArtifactName }}\"\n47-    dependencies:\n48-      - formula: git\n49-    caveats: |\n50-      ard runs agents that need the claude CLI and either CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN\n51-      or an API key in the environment.\n52-    # Accepted risk (see docs/proposal.md): the binary is not signed or notarized\n53-    # yet, so Gatekeeper's quarantine flag is cleared after install.\n54-    hooks:\n55-      post:\n56-        install: |\n57-          system_command \"/usr/bin/xattr\",\n58-                         args: [\"-dr\", \"com.apple.quarantine\", \"#{staged_path}/ard\"]\n139:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n274:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is edited after admission, the issue is re-gated with `ard:needs-human` and a comment until a maintainer re-applies the intake label. App-authored issues are exempt from the hash check. The loop never waits for a job: each claimed job is handed to a watcher goroutine that closes out through the shared Finisher, and a restart leaves running jobs to pool adoption and the Reaper. The re-gate hash covers the title and the body without ard's own cost block (see Guardrails, \"Intake is configurable\"), so ard's table writes never re-gate an issue. The first time ard writes the block, the stored hash stays valid; a maintainer relabel that falls between a human edit and ard's next table write may need repeating, because an ard body write bumps the issue's `updated_at`, which fails safe.\n525:## Guardrails\n551:- **The tag-writing credential is separate.** Only the `tag` job of `version.yml`, in the `release` environment, holds a token that can push tags, from a release App that must differ from ard's App (the job refuses otherwise). Tags are never moved or deleted. The logic is inline bash under `.github/`, which agents cannot change, and `internal/versioncheck` tests it.\n606:    P4 --> P5[\"Phase 5: Distribution<br/>versioned releases<br/>Homebrew cask\"]\n609:Phases 4 and 5 have no gate: Phase 4 ([#178](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/178)) writes the user documentation and publishes it as a GitHub Pages site, and Phase 5 ([#177](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/177)) publishes versioned releases and a Homebrew cask (`brew install --cask albertcbrown/tap/ard`).\n631:- [x] **Kubernetes executor ([#54](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/54)).** Decided 2026-11-24, design revision 3 ([design comment](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/54#issuecomment-7131825898), recorded in [#312](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/312)): no job API on `ard serve`; the controller pulls the result from a `post` container with a per-pod key; plain HTTP inside the cluster; stdout from a tee file, with forgeable usage figures accepted as a risk; a public `ard-agent` image; cluster settings are flags, not config; `flock` on a `ReadWriteOncePod` volume; size defaults as in Kubernetes-executor details; trufflehog off under k8s. See Kubernetes-executor details and Guardrails.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7eb1afcb-ed8c-476d-bb5f-6257ca4c1350","uuid":"30d5e657-7d97-465a-acd0-284e06b7c5be","timestamp":"2026-11-26T19:06:02.320Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Release runbook\n\n`.github/workflows/version.yml` tags every first-parent merge to `main` as `vX.Y.Z` and creates a notes-only release. This runbook covers setup and recovery. Names come from the workflow.\n\n## Seed tag\n\nAn admin creates the annotated seed tag once, on a commit on `main`:\n\n```\ngit tag -a v0.1.0 <sha> -m v0.1.0\ngit push origin refs/tags/v0.1.0\n```\n\nUntil it exists a dry run plans nothing and a live run fails.\n\n## Settings checklist\n\n- Repository variables: `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (ard's App login, `<slug>[bot]`), `ARD_VERSIONING` (`on` for live), `RELEASE_APP_ID`, `RELEASE_BOT_NAME`, `RELEASE_BOT_EMAIL`.\n- A `release` environment holding the secret `RELEASE_APP_PRIVATE_KEY`. The release App needs contents: write only, and must not be ard's App.\n- A tag ruleset on `v*` that only the release App may bypass.\n- For Homebrew: the secret `TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN` in the same `release` environment, and the `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap` repository (see Homebrew release).\n\nThe steps below set these up once. They use `ard-release` as the App's name; use any name that is free, and substitute it throughout.\n\n## Create the release App\n\n`version.yml`'s `tag` job mints a short-lived token from this App (`actions/create-github-app-token`) to push tags and create releases. It is a separate App from ard's so that ard's own token can never create a tag; the job refuses to run if the two are the same (it compares the App's slug with `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`).\n\n1. Open <https://github.com/settings/apps/new> (for an organization: the organization's Settings → Developer settings → GitHub Apps → New GitHub App).\n   - **GitHub App name:** `ard-release`. **Homepage URL:** the repository URL.\n   - **Webhook:** clear **Active**. The App receives no events.\n   - **Repository permissions:** **Contents: Read and write**. Leave every other permission at **No access** (Metadata stays read-only, as GitHub requires).\n   - **Where can this GitHub App be installed?** **Only on this account**.\n   - Select **Create GitHub App**.\n2. On the App's page, note the **App ID**. Under **Private keys**, select **Generate a private key**; the browser downloads a `.pem` file.\n3. Select **Install App**, choose the account, then **Only select repositories** → `albertcbrown/ard`. Install it on nothing else.\n4. Set the repository variables. The tagger email is the bot's noreply address, which needs the bot account's numeric user ID:\n\n   ```\n   gh api '/users/ard-release[bot]' -q .id\n   gh variable set RELEASE_APP_ID    -R albertcbrown/ard -b '<app-id>'\n   gh variable set RELEASE_BOT_NAME  -R albertcbrown/ard -b 'ard-release[bot]'\n   gh variable set RELEASE_BOT_EMAIL -R albertcbrown/ard -b '<bot-user-id>+ard-release[bot]@users.noreply.github.com'\n   ```\n\n   Check that `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` is also set to ard's own App login (`gh variable list -R albertcbrown/ard`).\n5. Create the `release` environment and store the key only there, then delete the downloaded file:\n\n   ```\n   gh api -X PUT repos/albertcbrown/ard/environments/release\n   gh secret set RELEASE_APP_PRIVATE_KEY -R albertcbrown/ard --env release < ~/Downloads/ard-release.<date>.private-key.pem\n   rm ~/Downloads/ard-release.<date>.private-key.pem\n   ```\n\n   Optionally restrict the environment's deployment branches and tags to `main` and `v*` (Settings → Environments → release), so only runs from those refs can read its secrets.\n\nTo rotate the key, generate a new one on the App page, run the `gh secret set` line again with it, then delete the old key on the App page.\n\n## Protect the tags\n\nA tag ruleset keeps tags immutable and lets only the release App create them. `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`'s App, workflows using `GITHUB_TOKEN` and people without bypass cannot create, move or delete a `v*` tag.\n\n```\ngh api -X POST repos/albertcbrown/ard/rulesets --input - <<'EOF'\n{\n  \"name\": \"release tags\",\n  \"target\": \"tag\",\n  \"enforcement\": \"active\",\n  \"conditions\": { \"ref_name\": { \"include\": [\"refs/tags/v*\"], \"exclude\": [] } },\n  \"rules\": [ { \"type\": \"creation\" }, { \"type\": \"update\" }, { \"type\": \"deletion\" } ],\n  \"bypass_actors\": [ { \"actor_id\": <app-id>, \"actor_type\": \"Integration\", \"bypass_mode\": \"always\" } ]\n}\nEOF\n```\n\n`actor_id` is the release App's **App ID** (the same number as `RELEASE_APP_ID`). The same ruleset can be made in Settings → Rules → Rulesets → New tag ruleset: target `v*`, enable **Restrict creations**, **Restrict updates** and **Restrict deletions**, and add the App under **Bypass list**. Check it with `gh api repos/albertcbrown/ard/rulesets`.\n\nThe seed tag (above) must exist before the ruleset, or an admin must be added to the bypass list while creating it and removed afterwards.\n\n## Turn it on\n\n1. Run a dry run and read the plan in the run's step summary:\n\n   ```\n   gh workflow run version.yml -R albertcbrown/ard\n   gh run watch -R albertcbrown/ard \"$(gh run list -R albertcbrown/ard -w version.yml -L 1 --json databaseId -q '.[0].databaseId')\"\n   ```\n\n2. Set `ARD_VERSIONING` to `on` (`gh variable set ARD_VERSIONING -R albertcbrown/ard -b on`).\n3. Run live, raising `max_tags` when the dry run planned more than 20 tags:\n\n   ```\n   gh workflow run version.yml -R albertcbrown/ard -f dry_run=false -f max_tags=60\n   ```\n\n   A failure that names a variable or the App points at the step above that sets it; fix it and rerun (see Recovery).\n\n## Homebrew release (release.yml)\n\n`.github/workflows/release.yml` runs on each `v*` tag `version.yml` pushes (and on `workflow_dispatch` with a `tag`). It builds the four archives with GoReleaser (`.goreleaser.yaml`), uploads them and `checksums.txt` to the tag's existing release, and updates `Casks/ard.rb` in `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap`.\n\n- It does nothing until `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on` **and** the `release` environment holds the secret `TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN`. Without the token it logs a notice and stops.\n- `TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN`: a fine-grained token (or a second App's token) with **contents: read and write on `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap` only**. Store it only in the `release` environment.\n- Create `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap` (public, with a `Casks/` directory) and protect its `main`.\n\nTo set up the tap and its token:\n\n1. Create the tap repository with a `Casks/` directory:\n\n   ```\n   gh repo create albertcbrown/homebrew-tap --public --description \"Homebrew tap for ard\"\n   gh repo clone albertcbrown/homebrew-tap && cd homebrew-tap\n   mkdir Casks && touch Casks/.gitkeep && git add Casks && git commit -m \"Add Casks directory\" && git push origin HEAD:main\n   ```\n\n2. Create the token at <https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens/new>: **Resource owner** `albertcbrown`, **Repository access** → **Only select repositories** → `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap`, **Repository permissions** → **Contents: Read and write**, nothing else. Pick an expiry and note it; the release stops with a push error when it lapses.\n3. Store it in the `release` environment only. `gh` prompts for the value, so it stays out of shell history:\n\n   ```\n   gh secret set TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN -R albertcbrown/ard --env release\n   ```\n\n4. Protect the tap's `main` (Settings → Rules → New branch ruleset: block force pushes and deletions). GoReleaser commits `Casks/ard.rb` directly, so do not require pull requests there.\n5. Rotate the token before it expires: create a new one and run step 3 again, then revoke the old one.\n- On a tag push only the newest `v*` tag is released, so a backfill of many tags publishes once. To build an older tag: `gh workflow run release.yml -R albertcbrown/ard -f tag=vX.Y.Z`.\n- One release runs at a time (`concurrency: release`, never cancelled).\n- Check locally first: `make release-check` (snapshot, publishes nothing).\n\n## Dry run to live\n\nEvery run is a dry run until `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on`. Check the plan in a dry run's step summary, then set the variable. `workflow_dispatch` is a dry run unless `dry_run` is false; `max_tags` (default 20) caps the tags one run creates, so raise it after reading the plan when a backlog is larger.\n\n## Bumps and the module path\n\nLabel a PR `semver:minor` or `semver:major`; with neither it is a patch, and with both major wins. Go requires a `/vN` module path for N >= 2. A `semver:major` that would reach v2 stops the run (exit 2) until `go.mod` says `module .../v2`; relabel the PR or change `go.mod`, then rerun.\n\n## Recovery\n\n- A failed run stops at the first failure. Rerun it: the plan is rebuilt from the tags that exist, and a tag without a release gets a release-only backfill row.\n- Never delete or move a tag. If a tag is wrong, fix forward with the next version.\n- `ard version` prints the tag after `make build` on a tagged commit, and after `go install github.com/albertcbrown/ard/cmd/ard@vX.Y.Z`.\n# Install\n\nard runs on macOS and Linux. It needs:\n\n- Go (to build), `make` and `git` 2.47.0 or newer. An older git fails some fetches.\n- The `claude` CLI for the default agent.\n- Docker, only for roles that ship a `Dockerfile` (the cve, docs and lint scanners).\n\nBuild from source:\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard\ncd ard\nmake build\n./bin/ard version\n```\n\nPut `bin/ard` on your `PATH`. A Homebrew cask is planned (#177); this page will point to it once it lands.\n9:## How it works\n26:## Configuration by convention\n42:## Getting started\n46:## Development\n58:## License\n# Release: build ard with GoReleaser and attach the archives and checksums to\n# the notes-only release version.yml created for a v* tag, then update the\n# Homebrew cask in albertcbrown/homebrew-tap.\n#\n# Inert until switched on: it does nothing unless the repository variable\n# ARD_VERSIONING is \"on\" and the release environment holds TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN\n# (a token that can push to albertcbrown/homebrew-tap only). Without the token\n# it says so and stops; it never half-publishes.\n#\n# Only the newest v* tag is released on a tag push, so version.yml's backfill\n# of many historical tags does not publish one release per tag. Use\n# workflow_dispatch with a tag to build an older one on purpose.\n#\n# Settings and recovery: docs/runbooks/release.md. Design: docs/proposal.md\n# (Delivery) and #177.\nname: release\n\non:\n  push:\n    tags: ['v*']\n  workflow_dispatch:\n    inputs:\n      tag:\n        description: Tag to release (vX.Y.Z); must already have a release\n        required: true\n        type: string\n\n# One release at a time; never cancel one that is uploading.\nconcurrency:\n  group: release\n  cancel-in-progress: false\n\npermissions:\n  contents: read\n\njobs:\n  release:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 30\n    environment: release\n    permissions:\n      contents: write # upload assets to the existing release only\n    env:\n      ENABLED: ${{ vars.ARD_VERSIONING }}\n      EVENT: ${{ github.event_name }}\n      PUSHED_TAG: ${{ github.ref_name }}\n      INPUT_TAG: ${{ inputs.tag }}\n      HAS_TAP_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN != '' }}\n    steps:\n      - name: Decide\n        id: decide\n        shell: bash\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          skip() { echo \"::notice title=release::$1\"; echo \"run=false\" >>\"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"; exit 0; }\n          [[ \"${ENABLED:-}\" == on ]] || skip \"ARD_VERSIONING is not on; nothing to release\"\n          [[ \"${HAS_TAP_TOKEN:-}\" == true ]] || skip \"the release environment has no TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN; create the tap token first (docs/runbooks/release.md)\"\n          if [[ \"$EVENT\" == workflow_dispatch ]]; then tag=$INPUT_TAG; else tag=$PUSHED_TAG; fi\n          [[ \"$tag\" =~ ^v(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)$ ]] || { echo \"::error title=release::not a vX.Y.Z tag: $tag\"; exit 2; }\n          echo \"tag=$tag\" >>\"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n          echo \"run=true\" >>\"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n\n      - uses: actions/checkout@11d5960a326750d5838078e36cf38b85af677262 # v4.4.0\n        if: steps.decide.outputs.run == 'true'\n        with:\n          ref: refs/tags/${{ steps.decide.outputs.tag }}\n          fetch-depth: 0\n          fetch-tags: true\n          persist-credentials: false\n\n      - name: Newest tag only on push\n        id: newest\n        if: steps.decide.outputs.run == 'true'\n        shell: bash\n        env:\n          TAG: ${{ steps.decide.outputs.tag }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          newest=$(git tag -l 'v*' | grep -E '^v(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)$' | sort -V | tail -n 1)\n          if [[ \"$EVENT\" == push && \"$TAG\" != \"$newest\" ]]; then\n            echo \"::notice title=release::$TAG is not the newest tag ($newest); skipping (dispatch with tag=$TAG to build it)\"\n            echo \"run=false\" >>\"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n          else\n            echo \"run=true\" >>\"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n          fi\n\n      - name: Release exists\n        if: steps.newest.outputs.run == 'true'\n        shell: bash\n        env:\n          GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}\n          TAG: ${{ steps.decide.outputs.tag }}\n        run: |\n          set -euo pipefail\n          gh release view \"$TAG\" -R \"$GITHUB_REPOSITORY\" --json tagName >/dev/null \\\n            || { echo \"::error title=release::no release for $TAG; version.yml creates it first\"; exit 2; }\n\n      - uses: actions/setup-go@d8cbb8045d7f43706b9177cceb36c1f91a6644d5 # v7.1.0\n        if: steps.newest.outputs.run == 'true'\n        with:\n          go-version-file: go.mod\n          cache: false\n\n      - uses: goreleaser/goreleaser-action@a4f02ab5355a589de60d917a659ce617ed19b3de # v7.3.0\n        if: steps.newest.outputs.run == 'true'\n        with:\n          distribution: goreleaser\n          version: v2.20.3\n          args: release --clean\n        env:\n          GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}\n          TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN }}\n          GORELEASER_CURRENT_TAG: ${{ steps.decide.outputs.tag }}\n27-\n28-# version.yml already created the (notes-only) release; add files to it.\n29:release:\n30-  mode: keep-existing\n31-  github:\n32-    owner: albertcbrown\n33-    name: ard\n34-\n35:homebrew_casks:\n36-  - name: ard\n37-    directory: Casks\n38-    homepage: https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard\n39-    description: Autonomous repository development agents driven by GitHub issues\n40-    # The token comes from the environment of the release workflow.\n41-    repository:\n42-      owner: albertcbrown\n43-      name: homebrew-tap\n44-      token: \"{{ .Env.TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN }}\"\n45-    url:\n46-      template: \"https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/releases/download/{{ .Tag }}/{{ .ArtifactName }}\"\n47-    dependencies:\n48-      - formula: git\n49-    caveats: |\n50-      ard runs agents that need the claude CLI and either CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN\n51-      or an API key in the environment.\n52-    # Accepted risk (see docs/proposal.md): the binary is not signed or notarized\n53-    # yet, so Gatekeeper's quarantine flag is cleared after install.\n54-    hooks:\n55-      post:\n56-        install: |\n57-          system_command \"/usr/bin/xattr\",\n58-                         args: [\"-dr\", \"com.apple.quarantine\", \"#{staged_path}/ard\"]\n139:A worker's inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and, on a fix step, `test-output.log` (written by the loop, never committed) and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`. Nobody may change `issue.md` (only the scanner writes it, through the committer, not close-out), another role's file, any other file, or delete `plan.md`. A job that owns a `plan.md` section may add, replace in place, move or remove that section; a change to the text before the first `##` heading is allowed only when the job creates the file. Headings match exactly (case-sensitive), and `##` lines inside a fenced code block are text, not headings. A change outside the allowlist fails the job as `needs-human`: the comment names up to three offending paths, and only the redacted log is committed, never the offending files. A job with no valid role file still fails as `failed`, because the allowlist is checked after the role file parses. `logs/` is the controller's: files an agent writes there are ignored, not committed. `costs/` is the controller's too, but stricter: no role may write it, so an agent write there breaks the allowlist like any other path and fails the job as `needs-human`; the controller stages its own `costs/<job-id>.json` after that check, and it is never on `closeout.WritableFor`. The allowlist is code (`closeout.WritableFor`), not config; `plan_section` only names the heading a role owns. Workers and reviewers get their inputs outside `.ard/<issue#>/` (for example `.ard-input/<issue#>/`), because anything they write under it counts as their change. `issue.md` is the issue body without ard's cost block (see Guardrails), so the table never reaches an agent; the reviewer's untrusted `.ard/<issue#>/` input includes `costs/*.json`, which is controller-written and holds no free text.\n274:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is edited after admission, the issue is re-gated with `ard:needs-human` and a comment until a maintainer re-applies the intake label. App-authored issues are exempt from the hash check. The loop never waits for a job: each claimed job is handed to a watcher goroutine that closes out through the shared Finisher, and a restart leaves running jobs to pool adoption and the Reaper. The re-gate hash covers the title and the body without ard's own cost block (see Guardrails, \"Intake is configurable\"), so ard's table writes never re-gate an issue. The first time ard writes the block, the stored hash stays valid; a maintainer relabel that falls between a human edit and ard's next table write may need repeating, because an ard body write bumps the issue's `updated_at`, which fails safe.\n525:## Guardrails\n551:- **The tag-writing credential is separate.** Only the `tag` job of `version.yml`, in the `release` environment, holds a token that can push tags, from a release App that must differ from ard's App (the job refuses otherwise). Tags are never moved or deleted. The logic is inline bash under `.github/`, which agents cannot change, and `internal/versioncheck` tests it.\n606:    P4 --> P5[\"Phase 5: Distribution<br/>versioned releases<br/>Homebrew cask\"]\n609:Phases 4 and 5 have no gate: Phase 4 ([#178](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/178)) writes the user documentation and publishes it as a GitHub Pages site, and Phase 5 ([#177](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/177)) publishes versioned releases and a Homebrew cask (`brew install --cask albertcbrown/tap/ard`).\n631:- [x] **Kubernetes executor ([#54](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/54)).** Decided 2026-11-24, design revision 3 ([design comment](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/54#issuecomment-7131825898), recorded in [#312](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/312)): no job API on `ard serve`; the controller pulls the result from a `post` container with a per-pod key; plain HTTP inside the cluster; stdout from a tee file, with forgeable usage figures accepted as a risk; a public `ard-agent` image; cluster settings are flags, not config; `flock` on a `ReadWriteOncePod` volume; size defaults as in Kubernetes-executor details; trufflehog off under k8s. See Kubernetes-executor details and Guardrails.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012MQspFanwvNyX8orGWjP3U","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
